 -0.026336669921875, -0.0283966064453125, 0.01242828369140625, 0.0015974044799804688, 0.00946807861328125, -0.00440216064453125, 0.01117706298828125, 0.0016613006591796875, 0.007061004638671875, -0.0186614990234375, 0.0050506591796875, 0.025848388671875, 0.02777099609375, -0.0026111602783203125, -0.01515960693359375, -0.003643035888671875, -0.0081634521484375, 0.0170745849609375, -0.0152130126953125, 0.007442474365234375, -0.00728607177734375, -0.030548095703125, 0.0184173583984375, 0.00185394287109375, 0.0192108154296875, -0.00118255615234375, 0.0257110595703125, 0.030548095703125, 0.004039764404296875, -0.0197906494140625, 0.0020427703857421875, 0.021575927734375, 0.00453948974609375, 0.01000213623046875, 0.01236724853515625, -0.0281982421875, -0.00356292724609375, -0.00867462158203125, -0.025115966796875, 0.0080108642578125, -0.01296234130859375, 0.02593994140625, -0.0450439453125, 0.0144195556640625, -0.03271484375, -0.0289764404296875, 0.01800537109375, -0.0355224609375, 0.0198974609375, -0.006214141845703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work is guided by the following research questions: (1) How robust are representative memory-based self-improving agent methods to stochasticity and task order? (2) What are the sources of fragility in agent memory banks, and how do they impact performance? (3) How does task and environment specification affect agent reliability? (4) What evaluation protocols and system design recommendations can mitigate observed fragility? Each research question is directly mapped to a corresponding contribution and is discussed in the relevant results and discussion sections (see Results: RQ1\u2014Section \"Variance and Task Order Sensitivity\"; RQ2\u2014Section \"Manual Memory Analysis\"; RQ3\u2014Section \"Ablation Study on Task and Environment Specification\"; RQ4\u2014Section \"Recommendations for Evaluation and Design\").\n\nTo address these questions, our main contributions are: (1) a systematic empirical analysis of the robustness of two representative memory-based self-improving agent methods (EMR and MAPO) to stochasticity and task order (addressing RQ1, see Results, Table 1 and Figure 2), (2) a detailed manual analysis of agent memory banks using a reproducible rubric (addressing RQ2, see Results, Table 3), (3) an investigation of the impact of task and environment specification on agent reliability (addressing RQ3, see Results, Table 2), and (4) concrete recommendations for evaluation protocols and system design to mitigate observed fragility (addressing RQ4, see Discussion). Unlike prior work, which typically reports single-run results on fixed task orders, we explicitly quantify variance, task order sensitivity, and the effects of underspecification, providing new insights into the reliability of these agents.\n\n**Background and Related Work**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature (e.g., Pritzel et al., 2017; Guu et al., 2017; Sun et al., 2018), and are representative of the two main paradigms in memory-based reinforcement learning: experience replay and memory-augmented policy optimization. We focus on EMR and MAPO due to their strong empirical performance and prevalence in prior work, as well as their contrasting approaches to memory construction and usage. Other approaches, such as meta-learning (Finn et al., 2017) and continual learning with regularization (Kirkpatrick et al., 2017), do not explicitly maintain a textual or trajectory memory bank and thus fall outside our scope. A more detailed comparison to related methods is provided in the supplementary material.\n\n**Experimental Environments**\n\nExperiments are conducted in two complex simulated environments designed to be representative of real-world sequential decision-making:\n\n1. **Multi-step Navigation Task:** This environment consists of a 10x10 grid world with randomly placed obstacles, variable start and goal locations, and optional collectible items. Each of the 20 tasks varies in obstacle density, required path length, and the presence of distractor items, with some tasks requiring detours or multi-goal navigation. The environment is stochastic: obstacle layouts and item placements are randomized per episode. This setting is representative of robotic navigation and logistics planning, where agents must generalize across diverse spatial configurations.\n\n2. **Compositional Instruction-Following Benchmark:** This environment presents agents with natural language instructions composed of multiple subgoals (e.g., \"Pick up the red key, unlock the blue door, and place the key on the table\"). Each of the 20 tasks varies in instruction complexity (2\u20135 subgoals), object types, and required action sequences. The environment includes distractor objects and ambiguous cues, requiring agents to parse and execute compositional instructions. This benchmark reflects challenges in language-conditioned control and household robotics.\n\nThese environments were selected for their diversity, complexity, and relevance to real-world applications, providing a challenging testbed for evaluating agent robustness and memory reliability.\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Experimental Setup*\n\nAgents are implemented using standard architectures: for EMR, a recurrent neural network (GRU, 2 layers, 128 hidden units) with a memory buffer of 500 entries; for MAPO, a policy network (2-layer MLP, 128 units per layer) with a memory of top-100 successful trajectories. Optimization uses Adam (learning rate 1e-3, batch size 32). Each agent is trained for 10,000 episodes per environment, with early stopping if the moving average success rate over 100 episodes exceeds 95%. Hyperparameters are selected via grid search on held-out tasks: for each agent and environment, we sweep learning rates {1e-4, 5e-4, 1e-3, 5e-3}, batch sizes {16, 32, 64}, and memory sizes {100, 250, 500} (for EMR) or {20, 50, 100} (for MAPO), selecting the configuration with the highest mean success rate on the validation set. Full implementation details, including code, random seeds, and environment generation scripts, are provided in the supplementary material. Supplementary Table 1 summarizes all hyperparameters and environment configurations.\n\n*Evaluation Protocols*\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs (10 independent seeds per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Task shuffling is implemented by generating a new random permutation of the 20 tasks for each run, ensuring that each agent experiences a unique curriculum. For each agent-method pair, we report mean and standard deviation of success rates across runs and task orderings, as well as 95% confidence intervals computed via bootstrapping (10,000 resamples). For statistical significance testing (e.g., comparing performance under default vs. shuffled task order), we use paired t-tests, assuming approximate normality of success rates across runs. Normality was assessed using the Shapiro-Wilk test (p > 0.1 for all comparisons); where normality was not met, we additionally report Wilcoxon signed-rank test results, which yielded consistent conclusions. Where multiple comparisons are made (e.g., across agent types and environments), we apply Bonferroni correction to control the family-wise error rate. All statistical methods and their assumptions are detailed in the supplementary material.\n\n*Manual Memory Analysis*\n\nTo better understand the sources of fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory banks after training. The analysis uses a rubric with the following detailed criteria, selected to capture key aspects of memory quality that impact agent performance:\n\n(i) **Completeness of task representation:** Does the memory entry specify all relevant task constraints and goals? For example, in the navigation task, a complete entry should include the start location, goal location, and any obstacles encountered. An incomplete entry might omit the goal or misstate the path taken. This criterion is motivated by the need for accurate recall and transfer to similar tasks.\n\n(ii) **Relevance of retrieved memories to current tasks:** Is the retrieved memory directly applicable to the current task, or is it unrelated? For instance, if the current task is to navigate from A to B avoiding obstacle X, a relevant memory would involve a similar route or obstacle, while an irrelevant memory might pertain to a different start/goal pair or lack obstacles. This criterion assesses the utility of memory retrieval for generalization.\n\n(iii) **Consistency of memory updates:** Are updates made after each episode, and do they accurately reflect new experiences? For example, if an agent completes a task with a novel strategy, the memory should be updated to include this trajectory; inconsistent updates might skip episodes or incorrectly summarize outcomes. This criterion ensures that the memory bank evolves to reflect the agent's learning.\n\nFor each agent, we randomly sampled 50 memory entries from the final memory bank after training, stratified across early, middle, and late training epochs (approximately 17 entries per epoch segment) to capture temporal variation. Two annotators with graduate-level training in machine learning and prior experience with memory-based RL independently coded the entries using the rubric above. Annotators underwent a calibration session on a pilot set of 20 entries to ensure consistent application of the rubric. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion: annotators reviewed each disputed entry together, referenced the rubric definitions, and reached consensus on the final label. This process was chosen to maximize reliability and transparency. Inter-annotator agreement (Cohen\u2019s kappa) averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement.\n\n*Ablation Study on Task and Environment Specification*\n\nTo systematically test the impact of task and environment underspecification, we conducted an ablation in which we incorporated additional information into the memory construction process. Specifically, we provided agents with detailed rubrics specifying explicit success criteria (e.g., for navigation: \u201creach goal location without entering forbidden zones and collect all required items\u201d) and richer environment feedback (e.g., textual feedback indicating which constraints were satisfied or violated after each episode). This information was operationalized by augmenting the memory entries with structured fields for each criterion and by providing agents with environment feedback logs during memory updates. The goal was to assess whether more explicit task and environment specification could reduce fragility. Full ablation results, including breakdowns by environment and agent type, are provided in Table 2 of the supplementary material.\n\n**Results**\n\nIn this section, we address each research question in turn, summarizing key findings and referencing relevant tables and figures for detail.\n\n*Variance and Task Order Sens"}}